{
 "id": "steering",
 "name": "Steering",
 "summary": "Gen 6 Camaro uses a ZF rack-mounted electric power steering (variable-ratio rack-and-pinion) bolted to the front cradle, a collapsible tilt/telescope column and a two-piece intermediate shaft. Race builds keep the ZF EPS rack (no bolt-in race rack exists), add a quick-release race wheel, and correct bump steer with adjustable outer tie rod ends.",
 "overview": "The steering gear is a ZF rack with the assist motor and controller mounted on the rack (GM: 'ZF rack-mounted electric, power-assisted and variable ratio rack-and-pinion'), turning circle 38.4 ft on the ZL1. The rack bolts to the front cradle; inner tie rods thread onto the rack bar and outer tie rod ends taper into the knuckle steering arms (the model places the rack ahead of the axle line - verify on your car). The column is collapsible with an electric column lock and steering angle sensor; a two-piece intermediate shaft passes the dash panel and runs beside the driver-side exhaust to the pinion.\n\nKeeping EPS in a race car: the controller needs a heavy 12 V feed and ground, an ignition/wake signal and GM CAN messages (vehicle speed etc.). With a standalone engine ECU/PDM, plan a strategy (retain the needed GM modules on a private CAN, or use a gateway/emulator) during electrical design. The ZL1 1LE shipped with 'power steering deflectors' for track use, indicating EPS heat is a concern - duct cool air to it and log supply voltage.\n\nRace cockpit: quick-release wheel (NRG, Works Bell, OMP, Sparco), a Gen 6-specific hub adapter, and button/horn wiring through a contact QR or coiled cord. Removing the airbag wheel requires properly disabling SRS. Rules (NASA CCR / SCCA GCR) commonly allow aftermarket wheels and quick releases; check class rules regarding retaining collapsible columns and airbags.\n\nCommon issues: torn rack boots from header heat, inner tie rod wear, U-joint pinch bolts not seated in spline grooves, lash in cheap quick releases, bump steer after lowering.",
 "buildNotes": [
  "Bolt rack to front cradle on the bench; center the rack.",
  "Install tie rods, set approximate toe (count threads).",
  "Install column on IP beam or cage dash bar; install intermediate shafts with rack and wheel centered.",
  "Wire EPS (phase 8): heavy feed, ground, wake, CAN.",
  "Fit hub adapter, quick release, race wheel and button wiring (phase 9).",
  "Set alignment toe and measure/correct bump steer with adjustable TREs (phase 11); recalibrate SAS if factory ABS/ESC retained."
 ],
